 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The method requires computing centroids and masses for each leaf cell, which is linear with respect to the number of voxels in the cell. The recursive refinement adds a logarithmic factor relative to the voxel spacing precision [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Low. It only stores the centroids and masses for the current cell and its intersection [S1, unofficial][S2].
-*   **Measured Performance:** On a 512x512x256 voxel 'Block' dataset, the method produced 127,188 triangles with average min/max angles of 33.1/89.6 degrees [S2]. This compares favorably to Dual Marching Tetrahedra (DMT), which produced 133,512 triangles with average min/max angles of 16.5/103.4 degrees [S2]. The method produces more homogeneous angle distributions and fewer degenerated triangles [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Robust to noisy volumetric data; sticks dual nodes to the volumetric surface, avoiding self-intersections common in QEF; produces high-quality triangle angles [S1, unofficial][S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires computing connected components or intersection properties within each cell, which can be computationally intensive for large cells [S2]. The recursive refinement adds overhead [S2].
-*   **When to use:** When working with noisy volumetric data or when high-quality triangle angles are required, and self-intersections from QEF are a concern [S1, unofficial][S2].
-*   **When not to use:** When computational speed is critical and the data is very clean, simpler methods like QEF or Marching Cubes may be faster [S2].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The method requires computing the centroid and mass of the intersection within each leaf cell. This involves iterating over the voxels in the cell. The refinement step is logarithmic with respect to the precision required (bisection). Overall, it is linear with respect to the number of voxels in the active cells [G].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the mass and centroid for each leaf cell. Minimal additional memory compared to QEF which requires storing quadratic forms [G].
+*   **Measured Performance:** On a 512x512x256 voxel 'Block' dataset, the method produced 127,188 triangles with average min/max angles of 33.1/89.6 degrees, compared to Dual Marching Tetrahedra (DMT)'s 133,512 triangles with 16.5/103.4 degrees [S2].
+*   **Strengths:**
+    *   Robust to noisy volumetric data [S1, unofficial][S2].
+    *   Sticks dual nodes to the volumetric surface, avoiding self-intersections common with QEF [S2].
+    *   Produces more homogeneous triangle angle distributions [S2].
+*   **Limitations:**
+    *   Requires integration/centroid calculation over the cell volume, which can be more expensive than simple edge intersection checks [G].
+    *   Relies on the quality of the volumetric segmentation; if the connected components are fragmented, the centroid may be inaccurate [G].
+*   **When to Use:** Use when the input volume is noisy or when QEF-based methods produce self-intersections or vertices outside the object. Ideal for medical imaging or segmented data where surface fidelity is critical.
+*   **When Not to Use:** Use QEF if the surface is extremely smooth and noise-free, as QEF may offer higher precision in ideal conditions, though Mass Point Centering is generally more robust.
 
 ## Background
-The method was proposed by Uribe Lobello, Denis, and Dupont in their work on adaptive surface extraction from anisotropic volumetric data using contouring on generalized octrees [S1, unofficial][S2]. It was developed as an alternative to Quadric Error Functions (QEF) for dual vertex localization in dual contouring schemes [S1, unofficial][S2]. The authors noted that QEF is a poor estimator for noisy surfaces and can localize nodes outside the object, causing self-intersections [S1, unofficial][S2]. The method builds on the concept of using barycentres of connected components to approximate the dual vertex position [S1, unofficial].
+The method was proposed by Uribe Lobello, Denis, and Dupont in their work on adaptive surface extraction from anisotropic volumetric data using contouring on generalized octrees [S1, unofficial][S2]. It was developed to address the limitations of Quadric Error Functions (QEF) in the context of Dual Contouring, specifically regarding noisy data and self-intersections. The approach leverages the conservation of mass and moment to estimate the dual vertex position based on the geometric properties of the volume intersection within a cell [S1, unofficial].
 
